REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES:
====================

EXAMPLE 1: Identifying Objects in Photos
───────────────────────────────────────

AI Level:
- A system that can identify objects in images

ML Level:
- Manually define features (color, shape, texture)
- Train algorithm on labeled photos
- System learns pattern: if object has these features → it's a dog

DL Level:
- Feed millions of photos to neural network
- System automatically learns what makes a dog a dog
- No manual feature engineering needed
- Achieves 99%+ accuracy (like Google Photos)

---

EXAMPLE 2: Email Spam Detection
────────────────────────────────

AI Level:
- A system that can filter spam emails

ML Level:
- Manually define rules or features
- If email contains certain keywords → likely spam
- If from known spam sender → spam
- Train classifier on 100,000 emails
- System improves its detection

DL Level:
- Feed emails to neural network
- System learns patterns automatically
- Understands context, language, sender behavior
- Detects sophisticated spam that ML might miss

---

EXAMPLE 3: Self-Driving Cars
─────────────────────────────

AI Level:
- A system that can navigate traffic and avoid obstacles

ML Level:
- Use sensors (camera, radar)
- Manual features: distance to cars, lane markings
- Train algorithms on driving data
- Car learns when to brake, turn, etc.

DL Level:
- Deep neural networks process camera feeds
- Automatically learns to recognize: pedestrians, signs, lanes
- Uses multiple neural networks working together
- Much more sophisticated decision-making

---

WHEN TO USE EACH:
=================

Use AI when:
- You need general intelligent system
- You're designing a broad solution
- It's conceptual discussion

Use ML when:
- You have data and can define clear problems
- You need predictive models
- Features are well understood
- Computational power is limited

Use DL when:
- You have massive amounts of data
- Features are complex and hard to define
- You have powerful GPUs available
- Working with images, text, audio, or sequences
- You need state-of-the-art performance

---

SUMMARY:
========

• AI is the umbrella term for machines doing intelligent tasks
• ML is a way to achieve AI through data and learning algorithms
• DL is the most powerful subset, using neural networks to learn complex patterns

Think of it like:
- AI = The Goal (make intelligent machines)
- ML = A Method (learn from data)
- DL = The Most Powerful Method (neural networks learning automatically)

All DL is ML, all ML is AI, but not all AI is ML, and not all ML is DL.
